WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:03.840
Hey Wisecrack.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.840
Disembodied Jared here.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.040
I’m here today to talk to you about an issue
of grave importance.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.410
A dark stain on our generation.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.410
The Emoji Movie.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:16.160
Now, you probably know most of the reasons
to hate the Emoji movie: it served as a family-friendly

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.230
vessel to cram as many shameless product placements
as 90 minutes could fit.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:25.289
It’s also predictable, painfully unfunny,
and tries to convey a moral message as gracefully

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.080
as this.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.360
But there’s another reason you should hate
the Emoji movie; its politics.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.820
And that’s because, while it tries to tell
a story about inclusivity, it’s so poorly

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:43.109
thought-out that it manages to not only fail
at being inclusive, but accomplishes the exact

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:47.130
opposite- presenting us with a hopeless, totalitarian
nightmare.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.130
Seriously.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.980
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The Emoji
Movie.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.210
What Went Wrong?

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.960
And just in case anyone gives a s***- spoilers
ahead.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.520
But first, a recap.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:02.019
The Emoji Movie takes place in Textopolis,
a city of sentient Emojis inside some kid’s

00:01:02.019 --> 00:01:03.300
named Alex’s phone.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.299
Gene, a “meh” emoji, decides to stop mooching
off his parents and get a job: emoting for

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:09.740
his human overlord.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:15.100
Problem is, he has trouble maintaining his
meh-ness, expressing a range of emotions instead...

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.200
But since being “meh” is his function,
he fucks his job up, gets labeled a malfunction,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.860
"If you have expressions other then meh, what
you are is a malfunction,"

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.960
and evades deletion

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.820
from the textopolis fuzz.

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:33.469
As the movie drags on, his antics threaten
to kill him and everyone he loves once Alex

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:34.479
tires of his shit.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:36.380
"Wireless Wireless how may I help you?"

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.020
"I'd like to make an appointment.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.070
It's like this phone is playing games with
me."

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:44.600
His escape is assisted by a princess-turned-hacker
and a high-five who loves to dance.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:49.859
Alex decides to wipe his broken phone, "Are
you sure you want to delete everything?"

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:53.689
"Do it." and as Gene witnesses the deaths
of all his friends, which is definitely his

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:59.420
fault, he manages to malfunction with confidence
and grace, allowing Alex to properly mack

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.670
on some girl.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:06.039
In the end, Alex is happy with his broken
phone because it’s made him a mack-daddy

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:09.390
and Gene gets to express himself however he
pleases.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.550
The Emoji movie is certainly trying to espouse
a noble message: that we are not bound by

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.870
tradition or birth and can become whatever
we want.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:23.040
It’s not a new trope: the nobles overcome
their birthname for forbidden love a warrior

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.550
who wants to fight in spite of societal expectations
or pretty much any story where someone packs

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.250
their bags to escape their small town existence
for the big city lifestyle.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:38.160
It’s an easy enough concept, a concept you’d
have to try really, really hard to botch.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.360
And with that, the movie botched literally
every.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.360
single.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.200
opportunity.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.760
to sell that message.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:49.180
So much so it’s worth discussing before
we get into the totalitarian stuff.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.580
First, there’s Jailbreak.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.660
A princess emoji by birth, but she’d rather
do cool hacker stuff, like wear beanies and

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.700
watch Mr. Robot, instead of doing princess
stuff.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.680
Throughout the film, she assists Gene in escaping
to the cloud and evading deletion.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:07.260
She eventually saves the day by throwing away
the hacker costume, everting to a princess,

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.280
and whistling for the Twitter bird.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.040
In other words, you can grow up to be anything
you want, but when push comes to shove, you’re

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.170
going to have to sacrifice who you are in
order to become what society always expected

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.170
of you.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.520
It’d kind of be like the plot of Mulan but
if Mulan saved the day by going home and cooking

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.140
her father a nice dinner before folding his
laundry.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.440
Then there’s Gene, who was born a “meh”
emoji, but is capable of laughing, and screaming,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:33.750
and having sweet glasses.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.770
He faces discrimination by not falling in
line with the identity society has put out

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:38.280
for him.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.700
But a conversation between Gene’s parents
reveals that his father has the same malfunction:

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:48.960
suggesting that Gene’s malfunction is GENEtic
- get it? GENE?

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:55.200
Regardless of whether or not Emoji’s have
DNA , the idea that Gene’s whole identity

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.340
is inherited further undercuts the message
that society and birth shouldn’t dictate

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.740
what we aspire to be.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.780
And, because bad decisions come in threes,
to further cement the message that we all

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:12.250
contain some innate, inescapable nature by
birth, the hi-five emoji reminds us why he

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:17.100
has to press buttons "I’m a hand it’s
a big red button," and eventually saves the

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.039
day by performing the task he was born to
do.

00:04:20.039 --> 00:04:21.069
“Hand, button.”

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:25.819
Now, that’s not to say the movie can’t
be trying to teach people to have confidence

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:31.280
in the things they can’t change about themselves,
but it also manages to more or less demonstrate

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.660
that “You can achieve greatness if your
genetics allow it.”

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.650
It also manages to turn internet trolls, who,
in real life are horrible people by their

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:51.060
own volition, into an identity one is born
into, and therefore cannot choose.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.219
So instead we get the equivalent of a racial
underclass subject to constant denigration.

00:04:56.219 --> 00:04:57.530
So much for inclusion.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.889
But even if we wanted to concede that the
Emoji movie really was about being comfortable

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:06.780
in your identity, there’s another, scarier,
problem: it offers us a vision of the future,

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:12.139
that, to paraphrase George Orwell’s 1984,
is pretty much a “boot stamping on an emoji

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.100
face, forever.”

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:18.720
On one level, The Emoji Movie shows us a authoritarian
nightmare and its emphatic fuhrer: Smiler.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.760
Emojis are put into the service of the messaging
app, and anything that interferes with the

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.610
functioning of that app must be eliminated.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:30.270
While Gene supposedly overthrows this order
by flaunting his God-given talents, what can

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.169
we infer really happened to Textopolis afterwards?

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:37.409
To figure that out, we need a little help
from German philosopher Max Horkheimer.

00:05:37.409 --> 00:05:41.330
Horkheimer wrote extensively about an idea
called “instrumental rationality,” or

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:45.180
“instrumental reason” and believed it
played a crucial role in the domination of

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.740
humanity throughout the 20th century, especially
in totalitarian regimes.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.120
Instrumental rationality is the kind of reason
we use to achieve a particular end.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.979
If I’ve decided the Wisecrack team needs
eat 50 pounds of pizza, instrumental reason

00:05:58.979 --> 00:06:03.819
can help me figure out the best, most efficient
way for them to fill their bodies with regret.

00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:07.960
Drink lots of water the day before, go for
toppings with high water content, and smoke

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.199
lots of ganja.

00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:13.659
But instrumental rationality is dangerous
because it doesn’t ask bigger questions

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:20.300
like: why am I trying to destroy my body,
or, why did I just spend $300 on pizza?

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.720
And while Horkheimer wasn’t talking about
pizza or other everyday uses of practical

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.990
reason, he noted that modern society was fueled
by this flawed kind of rationality.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.560
Always figuring out the fastest way to get
somewhere, never trying to figure out if it

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.180
was even worth it all.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.960
Textopolis seems to be run on this kind of
instrumental rationality.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:44.349
The whole society is organized for one purpose:
to deliver Alex the emojis he selects.

00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:48.749
"Here each of us does one thing, and we have
to nail it every time."

00:06:48.749 --> 00:06:51.831
There are never questions like: Why does Alex
get to pick the emoji

00:06:51.831 --> 00:06:54.199
or, hey, isn’t this technically slavery?

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:59.509
And there’s good reason: If they go on strike,
Alex will probably uninstall the app and murder

00:06:59.509 --> 00:07:00.639
them all.

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:04.530
This is the problem with anthropomorphizing
utilities in films where there are humans

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:05.530
present.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:09.139
It’s cute at first, but when you think about
it, it gets pretty horrifying.

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:13.599
The problem with instrumental rationality,
for Horkheimer, is that it aids in the domination

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:14.599
of humanity.

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:18.620
Since it’s concerned with questions like
“efficiency” and “usefulness,” both

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:23.090
nature and humanity become inputs in the assembly
line of society.

00:07:34.309 --> 00:07:38.590
And when everyone is reduced to a use value,
things can get even scarier.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:43.349
It’s for this reason Horkheimer and his
fellow Frankfurt School thinkers characterized

00:07:43.349 --> 00:07:48.879
the totalitarianism of the 1930s as the ultimate
danger of the instrumental rationality that

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:50.680
pervades our modern world.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:54.159
We see the totalitarian danger of this everywhere
in the Emoji movie.

00:07:54.159 --> 00:07:58.699
Society is organized based on how individual
emojis can be instrumentalized in the service

00:07:58.699 --> 00:07:59.699
of Alex.

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:04.110
Emojis who are used often get to go to the
cool parties, are popular with their peers,

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.180
while those unused get sent to the loser lounge.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.729
And those that disturb the peace, as with
Gene, get liquidated.

00:08:10.729 --> 00:08:14.729
And while the existential threat of getting
deleted may sound like a good rationalization

00:08:14.729 --> 00:08:18.889
for killing Gene, it’s also worth noting
that most authoritarian regimes sell their

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:20.830
message through existential threats.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:25.160
You know, the whole “It’s either this
or the demise of the German people” schtick.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.470
So was Gene’s victory in the climax of the
film a blow against the authoritarian terror

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:30.479
of Smiler?

00:08:30.479 --> 00:08:35.010
Well, no: While, Gene is a revered hero, Hi-Five
is popular again.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:39.970
"Back on top of the hand pile," and the exclusive
VIP parties of the old-regime are now open

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:40.970
to all.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.840
"From now on, everyone is welcome!"

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.700
However, in the after credits scene, we see
Smiler relegated to the loser lounge, confirming

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:58.310
a still-existing hierarchy between usable
and unusable emojis.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.200
But for Horkheimer, this shouldn’t be much
of a surprise.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.460
As he writes,

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:15.080
In other words, the happiness of everyone
in Textopolis is only significant in that

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.660
Alex’s texting app still works.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:21.840
Nothing is solved in regards to the extreme
instrumental rationality of Textopolis.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:27.000
Emojis are still instrumentalized for Alex,
and if Gene’s malfunction is now popular,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.570
it is only by the whims of their human overlord.

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:34.650
We even get an Emoji dance where emojis are
seemingly free to emote as they please, but

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:40.520
this expressiveness is only permissible insofar
as it doesn’t threaten Alex’s social prowess.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:46.030
And because the film hints that Gene’s malfunction
is inherited, the implication is a new dystopia

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:50.930
where those with a quasi-genetic mutation
can better serve Alex while those born of

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.360
lesser genes can still be discarded.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.160
So while we may have thought that the end
of Smiler’s regime would have meant the

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:02.080
liberation of emotions in Textopolis, the
fact that Alex is the ultimate judge of functionality

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.010
means nothing has changed.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:08.400
Basically- the emojis still live under the
iron fist of Alex, and because nothing has

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:13.140
been done to undermine their instrumentalization,
what will become of Gene when Alex tires of

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.520
his ultimately meaningless emoting.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.370
One day he may decide to do away with whatever
money eyes slash kissing face slash sunglasses

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:23.330
emoji means and go straight for the eggplant
emoji.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:29.120
As if to foreshadow this, Hi-Five tells us
early in the film: “What you need are fans!

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:34.790
They give you complete unrelenting support
as long as you’re on top.”

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:39.650
And here’s the issue with making emojis,
which are things with an actual function,

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:43.920
a metaphor for humans, who are more than a
utility on a smartphone app.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.530
It makes a lot more sense to delete an emoji
for not doing its job than murdering a human

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.030
for not doing its job.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:54.510
In Textopolis, if the system truly goes too
far, Alex can just get another phone.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:58.060
That’s not exactly how human civilization
works.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.530
And here we can see the shift in how domination
happens.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:06.150
Whereas a hundred years ago the system enforced
its rules with repression like Smiler’s,

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:11.520
our modern societies strengthen their rule
with liberation- or at least, liberation within

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:12.520
bounds.

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:16.330
As fellow Frankfurt Schooler Herbert Marcuse
wrote, many of the freedoms afforded to Western

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:20.960
civilization only served to make the repression
of those societies more efficient.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:25.020
We’re given the freedom of how to exist
within the system, but never the freedom to

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.520
escape the system.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:31.200
The post-almost-apocalypse world of Textopolis
certainly seems more permissive.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.780
But what has been omitted from the story is
the possibility of escape.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:40.030
Jailbreak once resisted the system by escaping
it, living outside the app, but now she’s

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.950
back in Textopolis, and in full Princess attire.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:47.760
The Cloud, then, represents the ultimate resistance
to the system, which has been brushed aside

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:48.840
by the film.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.590
And Gene’s heroics have only served to perpetuate
this cruel system.

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.710
Is the ultimate message of the Emoji Movie:
resistance is futile?

00:11:55.710 --> 00:12:01.190
We’re either subjugated by a strict totalitarian
regime, or we get to express ourselves freely,

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:03.290
so long as it serves the same master.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.400
In either cases, freedom is an illusion and
you ‘re inescapably a pawn in the system.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.400
Gee, f*** you all and thanks for watching. Peace!

